It’s a fair concern, but it is not a slippery slope in the sense that viewers have no way to know what they are watching.

Most films and major releases retain their original music, and distributors usually identify releases by format, restoration, cut, or platform. When music has been changed, it is often documented by fan databases, reviews, restoration notes, or the distributor’s release information—especially for well-known titles.

Also, “the director intended” can be more complicated than licensing alone: studios have long released theatrical cuts, director’s cuts, TV edits, censored versions, dubbed versions, airline edits, and restorations. Music substitutions are one more reason versions can differ, not a reason to assume every movie is unreliable.

For a film you care about, the practical approach is:

- look for an edition described as the original theatrical version or a director-approved restoration;
- compare release notes and reputable restoration/review sources;
- prefer archival, Criterion-style, studio restoration, or earlier physical editions when version fidelity matters;
- be cautious with heavily edited TV, airline, or low-cost streaming editions.

The original soundtrack remains the historical reference point even if a later licensed edition has a replacement cue.

Yes—it is nicer, and for most movies it is close to the lived reality: the song remains part of the film people know and remember.

The less alarming version of the legal reality is not “every soundtrack is a ticking time bomb.” It is:

- most established films keep their original music;
- many were cleared broadly or permanently;
- existing authorized versions do not suddenly disappear because a license question arises;
- occasional problems tend to affect a particular later release—such as streaming, an overseas edition, or a remaster—not the film’s entire history.

So you can reasonably regard the original soundtrack as part of a movie “for good” in the artistic, cultural, and historical sense. Licensing only explains why an occasional later copy may not perfectly preserve it.
